text:
Previously, we published a blog post on Jev vs. RPT where we shared how our research teams have shown that tabular foundation models can be few-shot deciders and that SAP-RPT-1.6 outperformed purpose-built decision models on real enterprise workflows. It was clear from the community that this is the direction enterprise AI should go towards. 
 Today, we are making this concrete by announcing SAP-RPT-Decisions , a new model in the SAP-RPT family built for discrete business decisions (e.g., order approvals, exception routing, procurement triage), and similar structured choices that keep the business . 
 If you are new to the SAP-RPT model family, our blog post on SAP-RPT-1.6 is a must read. 
 Why a few-shot decision model? 
 ERP systems run on thousands of small, precise decisions. For instance, where to route a request, whether to approve an order, how to classify a document, which exception path to take and more. All these decisions require a typed answer: yes or no, an option from a set, or a score on a scale. 
 Today, we can solve these tasks using large language models (LLMs), but these models can be slow, expensive, and occasionally produce hallucinated outputs. Zero-shot decision models, such as Jev, offer another approach: making decisions without requiring task-specific labelled examples. However, speed is not everything. For enterprise workflows, the ability to use labelled examples from your own business is also important. 
 Jev models are designed to operate without task-specific labelled examples, so they can make decisions based on general knowledge acquired during pre-training. While they can optionally accept examples for better ground, they are not built to consume them at scale. 
 SAP-RPT-Decisions takes a different approach: it uses labelled examples as the foundation for business decisions. Every decision is informed by your enterprise data and knowledge, supplied at inference time through examples such as historical approvals, past routing outcomes, and previous classifications. The model makes predictions by learning from the examples in a single forward pass without fine-tuning. 
 Critical business decisions should reflect your business context, not just the model’s general knowledge. A purchase order approval should reflect your approval history, and a routing decision should reflect your current team assignments. SAP-RPT-Decisions is built to absorb the volume and variety of structured business data that drives accurate decisions, and relevant examples help it handle the long tail of edge cases that define real enterprise workflows. 
 SAP-RPT-Decisions is built for enterprise decision workflows 
 Faster decisions at lower cost. SAP-RPT-Decisions is built for structured decision tasks. In internal testing on an invoice routing task, its observed latency was approximately one-fifteenth that of GPT-5.4 [1] and ran at a fraction of the cost [2]. These results highlight its potential for high-volume, latency-sensitive workflows. 
 Confidence-informed automation. Use probability and confidence scores to determine when to automate a decision and when to escalate it for human review. SAP-RPT-Decisions returns typed, schema-constrained answers, with probabilities for every question and an additional confidence value for choice and score questions. 
 A simpler path to integration . Define your state and questions, then provide labelled examples from your historical data. The model uses those examples at inference time, with no task-specific training or fine-tuning required. 
 How it works 
 SAP-RPT-Decisions introduces a new /systemone endpoint. You describe what to evaluate (the state), ask one or more typed questions about it, and provide labelled examples for the model to learn from. The model returns a typed answer per question. 
 Three question types are supported: 
 noul : A yes or no judgement, returning the probability the statement is true. 
 choice : Pick one from a set of named options, returning a full probability distribution and confidence score. 
 score : Rate on an ordered scale, returning a probability-weighted score with confidence. 
 You can ask up to 10 questions about the same state in a single API request, combining different question types as needed. The model supports up to 2,048 labelled examples per request. A handful of examples per category can significantly boost accuracy beyond competing decision models. Choose the context size based on data availability prediction quality, latency, and cost. 
 The simplified example below evaluates a customer review using three questions in a single request: {
 "model": "sap-rpt-decisions",
 "state": { "review": "Battery dies in an hour and support never replied." },
 "questions": {
 "is_negative": {
 "type": "noul",
 "instructions": "Is this review negative?"
 },
 "topic": {
 "type": "choice",
 "instructions": "Which topic does this review concern?",
 "criteria": {
 "Battery": "Charge, runtime or power issues",
 "Support": "Customer service and responsiveness",
 "Price": "Cost or value for money"
 }
 },
 "satisfaction": {
 "type": "score",
 "instructions": "Rate the customer satisfaction.",
 "criteria": ["Very unhappy", "Unhappy", "Neutral", "Happy", "Very happy"]
 }
 },
 "examples": [
 {
 "state": { "review": "Love it, works perfectly and the team was super helpful." },
 "answers": { "is_negative": false, "topic": "Support", "satisfaction": 4 }
 },
 {
 "state": { "review": "Too expensive for what it does." },
 "answers": { "is_negative": true, "topic": "Price", "satisfaction": 1 }
 },
 {
 "state": { "review": "Battery drains fast but otherwise fine." },
 "answers": { "is_negative": true, "topic": "Battery", "satisfaction": 2 }
 },
 {
 "state": { "review": "Completely useless, broke on day one and nobody helped." },
 "answers": { "is_negative": true, "topic": "Support", "satisfaction": 0 }
 },
 {
 "state": { "review": "Decent value and the battery lasts a full day." },
 "answers": { "is_negative": false, "topic": "Price", "satisfaction": 3 }
 }
 ]
} 
 The response contains a typed answer for each question, with probability values and, for choice and score questions, an additional confidence value: {
 "answers": {
 "is_negative": { "type": "noul", "noul": 0.91 },
 "topic": {
 "type": "choice",
 "choice": "Battery",
 "probabilities": { "Battery": 0.74, "Support": 0.21, "Price": 0.05 },
 "confidence": 0.61
 },
 "satisfaction": {
 "type": "score",
 "score": 0.54,
 "legend": { "0": "Very unhappy", "1": "Unhappy", "2": "Neutral", "3": "Happy", "4": "Very happy" },
 "probabilities": { "0": 0.6, "1": 0.3, "2": 0.07, "3": 0.02, "4": 0.01 },
 "confidence": 0.46
 }
 }
} 
 Your downstream logic can use these values to determine when to auto-approve a decision and when to escalate it for human review. For choice and score questions, the confidence value is derived from the full probability distribution . 
 Getting started 
 SAP-RPT-Decisions is now available on SAP AI Core. You can follow our step-by-step guide here to get the model deployed and the full API specifications can be found on Help Portal. 
 If you have been working with SAP-RPT models, the /systemone endpoint follows a familiar pattern. Define your input, provide examples, and call the API. The main shift is from prediction rows to a state-and-questions structure, reflecting the decision-oriented design. 
 If you do not yet have access to SAP AI Core, you can try out the SAP-RPT-Decisions model on our SAP-RPT Playground. 
 SAP-RPT-Decisions now available on SAP-RPT Playground 
 SAP-RPT-Decisions is one of our latest additions to the SAP-RPT model family and it also represents the next step in building the decision layer for enterprise AI. We will continue to push on prediction quality, decision accuracy, and the breadth of enterprise workflows these models can handle. As always, your feedback and use cases greatly help shape what comes next. 
 Happy deciding! 🚀 
 ------ 
 [1] In internal testing on an invoice routing task, observed latency was approximately 1,500 ms for GPT-5.4 and 100 ms for SAP-RPT-Decisions. Latency may vary with workload and deployment conditions. 
 [2] In internal testing on an invoice routing task using SAP AI Core, SAP-RPT-Decisions cost approximately one-twentieth to one-fortieth as much as GPT-5.4, depending on the number of context examples provided. Costs may vary with workload, context size, and applicable pricing.
